<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Östergötland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as East Gothland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Östergötland is a historical province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vattern">Lake Vättern</a>, with Linköping and Norrköping as its main towns. One of the heartlands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>, it holds the Rök runestone and Vadstena Abbey, founded by Saint Bridget. In the 12th and 13th centuries it was a power base of rival royal families in the making of the Swedish kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ostergotland.jpg" alt="Östergötland" width="320" height="729" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lapplänning (highlighting by Lokal_Profil) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverigekarta-Landskap_%C3%96sterg%C3%B6tland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Östergötland is one of the traditional provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, bordering Småland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a>, Närke, Södermanland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. In older English literature it is also called Ostrogothia. A fertile, largely agricultural plain, the Östgöta Plain, runs from west to east through its middle, while the south is hilly with countless lakes and the north is hilly and forested. Off the eastern shore lies an archipelago of islands and islets. The province is traditionally divided into two halves, east and west of the river Stångån, and the Göta Canal crosses it from the Baltic Sea at Mem to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vattern">Lake Vättern</a> at Motala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention of Östergötland, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> of Scandza, appears in the Getica of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>. Its traditions reach back into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> and the undocumented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, when the region had its own laws and kings. It kept its own laws, the Östgötalagen, into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and about 50,000 ancient remains of different kinds survive, among them 1,749 grave fields.</p><p>Östergötland belonged to the Christian heartland of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and the Sverker and Bjälbo dynasties were decisive in the consolidation of the kingdom. Vreta Abbey, dating from the early 12th century, was the first convent established in Sweden, and Vadstena Abbey became the dominant convent of medieval Sweden. Linköping received its royal charter in 1287 and Norrköping in 1384. Since the 13th century Swedish princes and princesses have been created dukes and duchesses of provinces, and since 1772 these have been honorary titles only.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Linköping is the largest city, with Norrköping second. Linköping Cathedral is the second largest church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and is very well preserved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Among the castles open to the public are Ekenäs Castle, one of the best preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castles in Sweden, and Vadstena Castle, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">Vasa dynasty</a> between 1545 and 1620. The Rök runestone, now beside the church in Rök, carries the longest known runic inscription in stone and is considered the first piece of written Swedish, marking the beginning of Swedish literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Östergötland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Svear of the Mälaren valley and Uppland, centred on the royal and cult site of Uppsala, appear in sources from Tacitus onward, while the Geats lived in Västergötland and <b>Östergötland</b> to the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their heartland lay in Västergötland and <b>Östergötland</b>, around lakes Vänern and Vättern.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-geats">Kingdom of the Geats</a></li></ul></section>
